<%# The progress chart, read aloud. #337's rule applied to #434's chart.

    The chart it sits under is `aria-hidden`, like every other canvas in this app, and the
    reason this one needs a table where the old chart on this page did not is worth stating:
    that chart drew one series -- the heaviest set each day -- and the full set list is
    directly below it, so a table would have been the same training said twice. This one draws
    what each block opened at, the goal, and the pace line to it, and **none of those appear
    anywhere else on the page.** Without this they would be visible only to somebody who can
    see a canvas.

    A row per date and a column per series, which is the orientation that reads correctly
    here -- and it is the opposite of _chart_series_table, deliberately. That one compares
    several lifts across the same weeks, so a reader wants "Back Squat, 145, 150, 155" and the
    lift has to lead. This one is one lift over time, so a reader wants "12 March: heaviest
    155, estimated 1RM 174, training max 168" -- the date leads, because the question is what
    was true on a given day rather than how two things compare.

    Dates rather than the evenly spaced labels the old chart used, because that is the whole
    change #434 makes: the goal sits at its own date in the future and the pace line runs to
    it, and a table of categories would lose the elapsed time the chart exists to show.

    Empty where a series has nothing on a date, which is most cells: a session is one date, a
    block opening is another, and the goal is one row on its own at the bottom. An empty cell
    is that absence and says so rather than printing a dash somebody has to interpret. %>
<details class="mt-2">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer text-xs text-gray-500 hover:text-gray-700">
    Show as a table
  </summary>
  <div class="mt-2 max-h-96 overflow-auto">
    <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left text-sm">
      <caption class="sr-only"><%= caption %></caption>
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-gray-300 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500">
          <th scope="col" class="py-1 pr-3 font-medium">Date</th>
          <%- series.each_key do |name| %>
            <th scope="col" class="py-1 pr-3 font-medium"><%= name %></th>
          <%- end %>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200">
        <%# Every date any series has a point on, in order. Built here rather than passed in
            because it is derived entirely from `series` -- two arguments that had to agree
            about the same axis would be two things to keep in step, which is the trap the
            volume table's `weeks` local already has to be careful about. %>
        <%- series.values.flat_map(&:keys).uniq.sort.each do |date| %>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row" class="whitespace-nowrap py-1 pr-3 font-normal text-gray-700">
              <%= date.strftime('%-d %b %Y') %>
            </th>
            <%- series.each_value do |points| %>
              <%# Through chart_number, which is #256's answer: these weights are numeric(7,2),
                  so Sequel hands back a BigDecimal and printing it raw gives 0.155e3 -- the
                  correct rendering of a BigDecimal and no use at all to a lifter. The chart
                  escapes this because Chartkick serialises to JSON; a table does not. %>
              <td class="py-1 pr-3 tabular-nums text-gray-900"><%= chart_number(points[date]) %></td>
            <%- end %>
          </tr>
        <%- end %>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</details>
